  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in Belgium?', a: 'Yes — education is compulsory to 18 but school attendance is not, a distinction protected by Article 24 of the Constitution. Each language community regulates it: Flanders through a declaration to AGODI and fixed Examencommissie checkpoints (basic certificate by the year the child turns 13, first-cycle secondary certificate by 16, in Dutch), the French community through an annual declaration and the CEB/CE1D/CE2D sequence. Missing or failing a checkpoint at the required age means school enrolment.' },
    { q: 'If we enrol with Smartious, do the Belgian declaration and checkpoint exams still apply?', a: 'Yes. An online or foreign school not recognised by your community is home education under Belgian law — the declaration and checkpoints apply whichever provider you use, and any provider implying otherwise is leaving out the most important fact about this market. We plan the community calendar and the Cambridge calendar together from enrolment, and the checkpoints are genuinely passable with planned preparation.' },
    { q: 'What are the Flemish checkpoints exactly?', a: 'Per the official Flemish guidance: register with the primary Examination Board by the school year your child turns 11; hold the certificate of basic education by the year they turn 13; register with the secondary Board by 15; hold at least the first-cycle secondary certificate by 16 — examined in Dutch. Late registration or failure at the required age means compulsory school enrolment.' },
    { q: 'What is changing in the French community?', a: 'Two things worth planning around: the FWB\'s May 2026 reform advances a requirement for a detailed pedagogical project and a yearly training plan with closer follow-up, and pass thresholds for the CEB, CE1D, and CESS rise from 50% to 60% from the June 2027 examinations. Continuous, documented curriculum — schemes of work, contact hours, progress records — is exactly what those requirements draw on.' },
    { q: 'We are a Brussels family — which community should we declare under?', a: 'It is a genuine strategic choice, usually decided by the language your child can be examined in: Dutch-language Examencommissie checkpoints at 13 and 16, or the French-language CEB/CE1D/CE2D sequence with the 2026 documentation requirements. We talk it through with every Brussels family before the first declaration.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare to the European Schools, ISB, and BSB?', a: 'The European Schools are excellent for entitled EU-staff families but oversubscribed for everyone else; ISB and BSB provide premium campus education at roughly EUR 30,000-46,000 in the senior years. Smartious delivers live online Cambridge, IB, and American curriculum at USD 2,160-6,480/year with immediate enrolment — most often as the waitlist bridge or the rotation-proof track for institutional families.' },
    { q: 'We are a SHAPE or NATO family — does Smartious fit a rotation?', a: 'It is one of the situations Smartious fits best: one curriculum held constant across every posting, with the same teachers and examination board from Belgium to the next assignment. Families in national-section or accredited base schooling should confirm their home-education status through their command\'s education liaison — and Smartious works either way.' },
    { q: 'Where do Belgium students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At Cambridge/Edexcel-authorised schools concentrated in the Brussels area, where Belgium\'s British-curriculum schools sit these examinations routinely. External-candidate capacity is confirmed per family per session.' },
    { q: 'Can my child access Belgian universities with A-Levels or an IB Diploma?', a: 'Yes — via equivalence recognition: NARIC-Vlaanderen for the Flemish institutions and the FWB equivalence process for the French-speaking system, with programme-specific requirements, entrance exams for restricted fields, and language proficiency for Dutch- or French-taught degrees. UCAS and Common Application routes remain open internationally.' },
    { q: 'How does live class scheduling work given the Belgium-Nairobi time difference?', a: 'Belgium runs CET (UTC+1) in winter and CEST (UTC+2) in summer — only 1-2 hours behind Nairobi EAT. Live afternoon Nairobi teaching lands naturally within the Belgian school day.' },
  ],

  ctaH: 'Book an assessment for your child',
